THIRD PARTY SW - MODIFIED
Unlike a plain redistribution, this project *modifies* third party sources: it
ports Arm assembly from ARMv7E-M (Cortex-M4) to ARMv7-R
(Cortex-R5F). Every modified file carries a notice describing the change -
required by the Apache License 2.0 section 4(b) for the files it covers, and
done for the CC0 ones regardless. These are all of them, and what was changed
in each:

  in crypto_sign/ml-dsa-44/r5f/ and crypto_sign/ml-dsa-44/r5fstack/

    ntt.S              the .syntax / .cpu / .thumb directives replaced by
    smallntt_769.S     include "pqr5f_arch.i"
    pointwise_mont.S

    vector.S           the same, and a byte-wise variant of the 24-bit sample
                         load in pqcrystals_dilithium_asm_rej_uniform, under
                         -DPQR5F_STRICT_ALIGN

    config.h           DILITHIUM_MODE wrapped in ifndef, so the parameter set
                         can be chosen from the command line

  in common/

    keccakf1600.S      the same architecture selection, and byte-wise variants
                         of the lane load and store in
                         __KeccakF1600_StateXORLanes and
                         __KeccakF1600_StateExtractLanes, under
                         -DPQR5F_STRICT_ALIGN

pointwise_mont.s and vector.s were additionally renamed to .S so that the C
preprocessor runs on them. Nothing else about them changed.

Where pqm4’s m4fstack holds a symbolic link into m4f, r5fstack holds a copy of 
the file the link points to, with identical content.

No other third-party file is modified. The rest of what came from pqm4 - the C
sources, the headers, and the two macro include files - is byte-identical to
upstream and carries no change notice, because there is no change to describe.
So is common/randombytes.h, which is PQClean's file copied as it stands; it is
here only because mupq/common/randombytes.h is a symbolic link into
mupq/pqclean/ that a Windows checkout does not materialise, which would leave
the include path with no such header at all.

That the instruction encodings are unchanged is checked mechanically against
the unmodified originals by tools/check_encoding.sh.

