THIRD PARTY TEST DATA - NIST
The FIPS 204 test vectors come from the National Institute of Standards and
Technology (NIST). NIST is the source of this data.

None of that data is redistributed here - not the files, not arrays generated
from them, and not a submodule pointing at them. NIST's repository is treated
the way the TI SDK is: it lives on your machine, and the build references it.
tools/gen_acvp.py fetches the three files below on request, after asking, into
a git-ignored cache, or reads a checkout you already have; it then generates
test/ml-dsa/acvp/acvp_vectors_{44,65,87}.c, which are git-ignored too.

Two things of NIST's origin are therefore in this repository, deliberately.
One is the locating information in this section and in tools/gen_acvp.py - the
repository URL, the commit, the three paths, their digests - which is what the
requirement to acknowledge NIST as the source amounts to in practice, and from
which no vector can be reconstructed. The other is NIST's notice itself,
quoted below because its terms require it to be kept intact.

Source:
  https://github.com/usnistgov/ACVP-Server
  commit 975de31eb83d87039ec88934fdc47d8c312b892d (RELEASE/v1.1.0.43)
  gen-val/json-files/ML-DSA-keyGen-FIPS204/internalProjection.json
  gen-val/json-files/ML-DSA-sigGen-FIPS204/internalProjection.json
  README.md (for the notice below)

The fetch is by commit rather than by branch, and each file is checked against
a sha256 recorded in tools/gen_acvp.py, so a build is generated from the
content of that one revision and not from whatever a URL serves later.

Changed from the NIST originals, in the generated files: 25 keyGen and 3
sigGen cases per parameter set are selected and rewritten as C arrays, and the
expected outputs are reduced to FNV-1a checksums instead of being carried in
full.

What follows between the two marker lines is NIST's own text, quoted. It was
written by NIST, not by the author of pqr5f, and it is included because NIST's
terms require this notice to be kept intact. Only
the line wrapping was changed to fit this file.

It is the complete License section of the ACVP-Server README at the commit
named above, which is the whole of that repository's terms, since it carries
no LICENSE file. tools/gen_acvp.py compares the quote against that README and
refuses to generate anything if the two have stopped agreeing word for word,
so moving the pin to a revision whose terms differ stops the build instead of
going unnoticed.

